ngths to see how the algorithm handles different widths.\n\nWith a narrower terminal width of 15, the four-column layout exceeds the limit, so it needs to split into two rows with three columns. I'm calculating the maximum width for each column—the first column with \"short\" and \"x\" has width 5, the second with \"medium\" and \"y\" has width 6, and the third column would be empty since there are only four entries. I'm seeing that even though the algorithm returns 3 columns, the actual grid only has 2 columns of real data—the third column would be empty since there aren't enough entries to fill it. That's acceptable behavior for this layout approach.\n\nLet me step back and test this with concrete examples instead. For a narrow terminal, I'd expect entries to stack top-to-bottom across 2 columns, but with a wide terminal, all 4 entries should fit in a single row. And if the terminal is too narrow to even fit a single entry, it should collapse to one column.\n\nNow I'll write out the test cases to verify this logic works.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3137,"cache_read_input_tokens":24655,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3137},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"30f6ce8b-19e5-4f5d-a66c-eb0ad304e850","uuid":"d5dbdc0a-8006-4907-b310-176f466436df"}
